L2WBA‑OS: A Deterministic Mediation Framework for Safe AI‑Driven Humanoid Robotics

Author: ouadi Maakoul 

Abstract

Humanoid robots operating in unstructured environments face a fundamental tension: AI brains demand open‑ended control authority, yet physical bodies require iron‑clad safety guarantees that no existing middleware provides. Control Barrier Function Quadratic Programming (CBF‑QP) safety filters can become infeasible under conflicting constraints; the Projected Safe Set Algorithm (p‑SSA) achieves feasibility by relaxing constraints but provides neither formal worst‑case execution‑time (WCET) bounds, a sovereignty hierarchy, nor a teaching signal for the AI brain. This thesis argues that safe autonomy for humanoids requires sovereign constrained projection: a total, priority‑respecting, time‑bounded mapping from arbitrary AI intent to certifiably safe actuation—always defined even when the safe set is empty or the QP infeasible. We present L2WBA‑OS, a deterministic, formally verified mediation layer deployed on a real‑time Linux kernel with core isolation and EtherCAT. Its contributions are: (1) a mathematical theory of total constrained projection with a six‑step fallback cascade whose WCET is bounded by static analysis; (2) a sovereignty hierarchy (HAL safety kernel ≻ Mediator ≻ Brain) verified for deadlock‑freedom and safety via UPPAAL timed automata and Lean 4; (3) a gradient‑based teaching signal enabling AI brains to co‑adapt, with a formal convergence theorem; (4) an FMEA‑based adversarial validation suite and ISO‑13849/ISO‑25785‑1‑aligned certification pipeline; and (5) a CORA‑grounded marketplace for plug‑and‑play brain‑body interoperability. L2WBA‑OS will be released as open‑source (Apache 2.0) to serve as the reference safety kernel for the next generation of humanoid robots.

---

1. Introduction & Central Thesis

Humanoid robots capable of whole‑body locomotion and manipulation are on the verge of industrial and societal deployment. The latest generation of generalist AI models—large vision‑language‑action (VLA) systems—enables open‑ended task specification, and the emerging paradigm of Behaviour Foundation Models (BFMs) now aims to replace traditional model‑based stacks with end‑to‑end neural policies that output direct joint commands. Yet the gap between a high‑level AI brain and a physical humanoid body remains the number‑one obstacle to safe, real‑world operation. A single ill‑posed command—requesting a velocity that violates ground friction, a torque that overheats an actuator, or a trajectory that causes self‑collision—can result in catastrophic failure.

Recent plug‑and‑play safety layers, notably those based on Control Barrier Functions (CBFs), filter AI commands via quadratic programming (QP). The VLSA/AEGIS framework targets VLA models with CBF‑QP; RoboNeuron provides middleware orchestration. However, CBF‑QP can become infeasible when multiple constraints conflict or when the safe set is non‑convex. The Projected Safe Set Algorithm (p‑SSA) addresses infeasibility by relaxing constraints in a principled manner, and has been validated on a real Unitree G1 humanoid. Critically, however, p‑SSA provides no formal WCET analysis, no sovereignty hierarchy, no teaching signal, and no certification pathway. Its relaxation techniques cannot bound safety violations when relaxation is needed.

The trend toward end‑to‑end neural BFMs exacerbates the safety problem: BFMs are black‑box function approximators that can produce physically infeasible outputs, and no finite‑sample learning algorithm can provide a uniform safety guarantee over a continuous state space. For any finite training set, there remain uncountable states arbitrarily close to training points at which the policy may violate constraints—a direct consequence of the density of unsafe states near the constraint boundary under mild regularity conditions on g_j. Thus a certified mediation layer remains indispensable.

Central Thesis. Safe autonomy for humanoid robots requires sovereign constrained projection: a mapping from arbitrary intent to feasible control that is (1) total—always defined even under infeasibility; (2) priority‑respecting—preserving stability over secondary objectives; (3) time‑bounded—computable within a proven WCET; and (4) formally verifiable. We prove that no single‑QP approach (CBF‑QP or p‑SSA) can satisfy all four properties simultaneously, and we provide a constructively complete alternative: L2WBA‑OS.

