2. Problem Statement (Formal)

Let a body be a tuple  \mathcal{B} = (\mathcal{Q}, \mathcal{U}, \mathcal{X}, \mathcal{F}) , where

·  \mathcal{Q} \subset \mathbb{R}^n  is the configuration space,
·  \mathcal{U} \subset \mathbb{R}^m  the control input space (joint torque/velocity),
·  \mathcal{X}  the state space (positions, velocities, temperatures, environmental parameters),
·  \mathcal{F}: \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{U} \to \mathcal{X}  the (possibly stochastic) dynamics.

An AI brain generates an intent  i \in \mathcal{I} , mapped by a brain‑specific adapter to a desired instantaneous control  \mathbf{u}_{\text{des}} \in \mathcal{U} . For BFM‑based brains that output direct joint torques, the adapter passes those torques as the intent.

Safety constraints are predicates  g_j(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{u}) \le 0 defining the safe set

\mathcal{S} = \{ \mathbf{u} \in \mathcal{U} \mid \forall j,\; g_j(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{u}) \le 0 \}.

Problem 1 (Safe Mediation). Given \mathbf{x} and \mathbb{C} = \{g_j\}, produce  \mathbf{u}^* \in \mathcal{U}  that

1. is always defined (totality), even if \mathcal{S} = \emptyset or the QP is infeasible;
2. minimally deviates from \mathbf{u}_{\text{des}} under a metric d;
3. is computable deterministically within \tau_{\text{max}} = 500\,\mu\text{s}.

Problem 2 (Sovereignty & Scheduling). Prove that a layered architecture (HAL safety kernel, mediator, brain) with real‑time scheduling guarantees no unsafe command reaches the actuators, even under deadline misses.

Problem 3 (Certification & Interoperability). Devise a quantitative compliance score aligned with ISO‑13849 performance levels and the forthcoming ISO‑25785‑1, and a marketplace protocol that matches brains and bodies using IEEE 1872 (CORA) capability ontologies.

The core scientific contribution is a theory of sovereign constrained projection.

